Agreed.

The review/recovery distinction is now much clearer to me and I agree they should be separate renderers rather than alternative presentations of the same artifact.

I also agree with removing modular as a user-facing mode and replacing it with automatic chunking plus a continuation contract.

On full versus delta, my intended use case is:

- full = complete portable snapshot
- delta = patch against an existing state

The reason I want delta is token efficiency when the effective state is already being carried forward manually.

One area I'd like to make explicit before testing is multi-chat continuity.

The handoff should compile the current effective state, not merely the contents of the current chat.

Example:

Chat 1 creates rule A.
Chat 2 creates rule B.
Chat 3 discusses only rule B.

A handoff from Chat 3 should still contain both active rules if both remain governing.

In other words, active state persists until superseded, regardless of which chat introduced it.

The current active/superseded ledger model appears compatible with this, but I want that behaviour to be considered intentional rather than incidental.

I also agree with:

- bootstrap restoring and holding rather than executing
- criticality-based schema ordering
- continuation manifests instead of predicted part counts
- review using markdown and recovery using XML

The portability point is important.

My target is cross-model and potentially cross-vendor portability.

A receiving assistant should be able to reconstruct utility tools from the payload even if it has never seen them before.

That likely means behaviour descriptions are more important than tag names.
